---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED, AUTHOR_NAME, CONTACT_EMAIL, AUTHOR_ROLE } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { personSchema, organizationSchema, breadcrumbSchema, profilePageSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { PAGES } from '../../lib/guides';
import { P } from '../../lib/engine/params';
const lang = 'en';
const n = PAGES.length + 1;
const nSrc = Object.keys(P.sources).length;
---
<PageLayout title="Pay Band Calc publisher: who works out NHS and teacher pay" description="Pay Band Calc publisher: who works out take-home pay and pensions for NHS staff and teachers in the four UK nations, where the figures come from, and how." lang={lang} jsonLd={[personSchema(lang), organizationSchema(lang), profilePageSchema({ path: route('about', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path: route('about', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">About</p><h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">Who works out the figures on Pay Band Calc</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <div class="not-prose mb-6"><p class="text-lg font-semibold text-navy-900">{AUTHOR_NAME}</p><p class="text-navy-700">{AUTHOR_ROLE[lang]}</p></div>
    <p>Pay Band Calc is published by Radif Partners, an independent company that builds free calculators on public rules. This one is for people paid on the two largest public pay scales in the UK: a healthcare assistant checking what a move from band 3 to band 4 is worth, a nurse comparing a post in London with one in Scotland, a teacher about to apply for the upper pay range, a trainee weighing the NHS against teaching, or anyone who wants to know what their pension will be. It has {n} pages, each with a calculator.</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Search for NHS pay scales and the first result is NHS Employers, which publishes the official 2026/27 scale and stops at gross pay. The calculators that rank next were, in October 2026, still showing 2025/26 figures under a 2026/27 title, using last year's pension tiers for Scotland and Northern Ireland, or serving a placeholder instead of a calculator. Teacher pay pages gave take-home "before pension and student loan", which is not what anyone is paid. None estimated the pension.</p>
    <p>So this site does better than the first Google result because it starts from the same official scales and works out, for every band and every pay point, the real take-home after income tax (Scottish rates included), National Insurance, the tiered pension contribution and a student loan, with the London supplement and the scales of all four nations, and it estimates the NHS or teachers' pension at retirement.</p>
    <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <p>NHS scales come from NHS Employers, the Welsh Government, gov.scot and the Department of Health in Belfast; teacher scales from the School Teachers' Pay and Conditions Document 2026, the SNCT, the Welsh pay document and the Northern Ireland determination; pension rules from NHSBSA, Teachers' Pensions, the SPPA and the HSC Pension Service; tax from HMRC. All of them sit in one parameter file, each with the document it was read in: {nSrc} official pages and documents, all read on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}. The engines are tested against figures those bodies print, and a failing test stops the site from being built. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists each rule and what is left out.</p>
    <h2>What we are not</h2>
    <p>We are not the NHS, a school employer, a union, a pension scheme or a financial adviser. We cannot see your payslip, your tax code or your pension record. The figures apply the published rules to what you type: your payroll and your pension scheme hold the figures that count, and decisions about your career or retirement are yours.</p>
    <h2>How the site is paid for</h2>
    <p>It is free and needs no account or email. We sell no product, take no commission and link to no adviser or lender. If advertising is added one day, it will never sit above a calculator and it cannot use what you type, because nothing you type leaves your browser.</p>
    <h2>Tell us when a figure is wrong</h2>
    <p>Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the nation, band or point, hours and the figure you expected, ideally with the circular or payslip line that shows it. The <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how corrections are made.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-navy-600">Sources last read on <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-GB')}</time>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
